---
intent: >
  Collapse the parseBrainAide implementation to the new two-field schema:
  shrink `BrainAideConfig` to `{ name, mcpServerConfig: { command, args } }`,
  move the read path to `.aide/config/brain.aide`, drop validation for the
  retired fields (`connector`, `rootPath`, `entryFile`, `tools`), reject any
  of those fields explicitly with a `malformed-frontmatter` reason naming
  every stale field found, generalize `interpolateArgs` to substitute any
  top-level frontmatter field (with `${name}` the only resolvable key today),
  and rewrite the test suite around the new schema.
---

## Project Structure

This is an additive/edit plan against an existing module. No new files; no
new exports. The module's external surface is unchanged: `parseBrainAide`
(default), `parseBrainAideFromString`, and `interpolateArgs` all stay
exported with the same names and same signatures. What changes is the
shape of the `BrainAideConfig` type they trade in, the file path the
default export reads, and the validation rules the parser enforces.

```
src/service/parseBrainAide/
├── .aide                         ← [existing, aligned] intent spec — already
│                                    rewritten for the two-field schema
├── plan.aide                     ← [this file]
├── todo.aide                     ← [existing, all 16 items resolved] alignment record
├── index.ts                      ← [edit] orchestrator + helpers
│                                    exports unchanged in name and arity:
│                                      default async function parseBrainAide(root: string): Promise<ParseBrainAideResult>
│                                      export function parseBrainAideFromString(content: string): ParseBrainAideResult
│                                      export function interpolateArgs(config: BrainAideConfig): string[]
│                                    behavior changes:
│                                      - reads `.aide/config/brain.aide` (not `.aide/brain.aide`)
│                                      - validates only `name` + `mcpServerConfig.{command, args}`
│                                      - rejects deprecated fields (`connector`, `rootPath`,
│                                        `entryFile`, `tools`) with malformed-frontmatter
│                                      - interpolation walks any top-level frontmatter field;
│                                        substitution map shrinks to `{ name }`
└── index.test.ts                 ← [rewrite] new fixtures + new validation tests +
                                     deprecated-field rejection tests
```

External edit (called out for the implementor; the change is small but the
file is shared with many other modules):

```
src/types/index.ts                ← [edit] collapse BrainAideConfig to the
                                     two-field shape; delete BrainAideToolMapping
                                     (no remaining consumers); delete
                                     BrainAideMcpServerConfig if it has no
                                     other consumers, OR keep it as the
                                     `mcpServerConfig` field's type. The
                                     `ParseBrainAideResult.ok` variant
                                     references the collapsed BrainAideConfig
                                     so its surface narrows automatically.
```

The single-file layout from the prior plan still applies. Both
`interpolateArgs` and the `parseBrainAideFromString` / `parseBrainAide`
pair fit comfortably in one `index.ts` and stay there. `interpolateArgs`
remains defined ABOVE the default export per the function-ordering rule.

## Prerequisites

This plan has no external module prerequisites. Every type and constant it
touches lives in files this plan edits directly (`src/service/parseBrainAide/`
and `src/types/index.ts`). The intent spec at `src/service/parseBrainAide/.aide`
is already aligned to the new schema (status: aligned).

**Downstream-breakage notice (not a prerequisite — informational):** Tightening
`BrainAideConfig` will break compilation in four consumers that still read
`config.connector` / `config.rootPath` / `config.entryFile` / `config.tools`:
`src/service/buildBrainState/index.ts`, `src/cli/init/writeMcpEntry/index.ts`,
`src/service/install/provisionBrain/index.ts`, and tests under
`src/service/install/provisionBrain/obsidianBrainAideTemplate/`. Those modules
have their own plan.aide files for the rename/refactor work; this plan does
not modify them. Step 6 (the verify step) runs `npx tsc --noEmit` and is
expected to surface those downstream errors. The implementor records the
list of failing files but does NOT fix them inside this plan — fixing them
is the next plan in the orchestrator's dependency chain.

## Plan

### 1. Collapse the shared types in `src/types/index.ts`

Read: `coding-playbook/workflow/type-system`, `coding-playbook/foundations/conventions`

- [ ] Edit `src/types/index.ts` to collapse the brain.aide-related types.
      The new shape is exactly what the spec's frontmatter declares — every
      retired field is removed from the type so consumers cannot reach for
      it without a TypeScript error.

      **`BrainAideConfig`** — narrow to the two-field shape:
      ```
      {
        name: string;
        mcpServerConfig: BrainAideMcpServerConfig;  // or inline { command, args }
      }
      ```
      Remove the `connector`, `rootPath`, `entryFile`, and `tools` fields.
      Update the type's JSDoc to reflect the new minimum schema and to point
      at the canonical doc (`.aide/docs/brain-aide.md`) as the format
      reference. The JSDoc must explicitly state that `name` is descriptive
      metadata never branched on, mirroring the spec's load-bearing rule.

      **`BrainAideMcpServerConfig`** — keep as-is (`{ command: string;
      args: string[] }`). Its JSDoc still notes that `args` may carry
      `${fieldName}` placeholders pre-interpolation; update the example
      reference from `${rootPath}` to `${name}` since `name` is the only
      resolvable field today.

      **`BrainAideToolMapping`** — delete. Its only consumer was
      `BrainAideConfig.tools`, which is also being deleted. Removing the
      type prevents any future caller from accidentally re-introducing a
      `tools` field by importing a type that hints at its existence.

      **`ParseBrainAideResult`** — no shape change at the union level; the
      `ok` variant's `config` field automatically narrows because it
      references `BrainAideConfig`. Update the union's JSDoc to mention
      that `malformed-frontmatter` covers deprecated-field rejection in
      addition to missing-required-field validation.

      Do NOT touch `BrainState` here — its `connector` / `rootPath` fields
      are owned by `buildBrainState`'s separate plan.

### 2. Update the read path constant in `parseBrainAide`

Read: `coding-playbook/foundations/conventions`

- [ ] Edit `src/service/parseBrainAide/index.ts`. In the default export
      `parseBrainAide(root)`, change the `brainAidePath` derivation from
      `join(root, ".aide", "brain.aide")` to
      `join(root, ".aide", "config", "brain.aide")`. Update the function's
      JSDoc summary to read `.aide/config/brain.aide` everywhere it
      currently reads `.aide/brain.aide`. No other behavioral change in
      this step.

### 3. Rewrite the frontmatter validation block in `parseBrainAideFromString`

Read: `coding-playbook/services/error-handling`, `coding-playbook/foundations/conventions`

- [ ] Edit `src/service/parseBrainAide/index.ts`. Replace the existing
      "Step 4: Validate required fields" block of `parseBrainAideFromString`
      with the new two-field validation. The block lives between the
      successful YAML parse and the prose-extraction step.

      - 3a. **Validate the required fields** in this exact order. Each
            failure returns `{ kind: "malformed-frontmatter", reason: <message> }`
            and short-circuits the rest of the function:
            1. `name` is a non-empty string.
            2. `mcpServerConfig` is a plain object (non-null, non-array).
            3. `mcpServerConfig.command` is a non-empty string.
            4. `mcpServerConfig.args` is an array AND every element is a
               string.
            The reason strings must NAME the failing field (matching the
            existing pattern: `"name is required and must be a non-empty
            string"`, `"mcpServerConfig.args is required and must be an
            array of strings"`, etc.) so consumers can quote them in
            remediation prose without re-parsing the message.

      - 3b. **Reject deprecated fields** AFTER the required-field
            validation passes. Build a list of deprecated keys present in
            the parsed frontmatter by intersecting the parsed object's
            top-level keys with the fixed deprecated set:
            `["connector", "rootPath", "entryFile", "tools"]`. If the
            intersection is non-empty, return
            `{ kind: "malformed-frontmatter", reason: "deprecated fields:
            <comma-separated list, in the deprecated-set order>" }`. The
            reason string names every stale field found in a single
            response so the user fixes them all in one edit. Examples:
            - one stale field: `"deprecated fields: tools"`
            - multiple: `"deprecated fields: rootPath, tools"`

            Order the listed fields by the deprecated-set order
            (`connector, rootPath, entryFile, tools`), not by the order
            they appeared in the user's file — deterministic output keeps
            tests and remediation messages stable across YAML key-ordering.

            Place rejection AFTER required-field validation (not before)
            so a user whose file is missing both `name` AND carries a
            stale `connector` sees the missing-`name` error first; once
            they add `name`, the next parse surfaces the deprecated
            field. This matches the existing one-error-per-parse pattern.

      - 3c. **Rebuild the validated config object** with only the two
            top-level fields. Drop the `connector`, `rootPath`, `entryFile`,
            and `tools` lines from the constructed `config` literal:
            ```
            const config: BrainAideConfig = {
              name: fm["name"],
              mcpServerConfig: {
                command: mcpServerConfig["command"],
                args: mcpServerConfig["args"] as string[],
              },
            };
            ```
            No other field appears.

      Update the function's JSDoc to mention deprecated-field rejection
      as a `malformed-frontmatter` sub-case alongside missing-required-
      field validation.

### 4. Rewrite `interpolateArgs` to substitute against the collapsed frontmatter

Read: `coding-playbook/foundations/conventions`, `coding-playbook/architecture/data-flow`

- [ ] Edit `src/service/parseBrainAide/index.ts`. Replace the
      `interpolateArgs` body. The contract is unchanged at the signature
      level (`(config: BrainAideConfig) => string[]`), but the substitution
      map shrinks to a single key.

      - 4a. **Shrink the substitution map** to `{ name: config.name }`.
            The previous implementation referenced `config.rootPath` and
            `config.entryFile`; both are gone from the type, so referencing
            them is a compile error and a lingering reference is the bug
            this step prevents.

      - 4b. **Keep the walk loop unchanged.** The existing
            `arg.replace(/\$\{(\w+)\}/g, ...)` pattern is correct — it
            already generalizes over any `${<key>}` placeholder by
            consulting the substitution map. Unknown keys (any
            `${something-not-in-the-map}`) pass through verbatim, which
            is the desired behavior: a `${rootPath}` placeholder left
            over from a pre-collapse brain.aide fails the deprecated-
            field check at parse time, so it never reaches the
            interpolator.

      - 4c. **Update the function's JSDoc** to reflect the new audience
            framing: substitution is "any `${fieldName}` against the
            top-level frontmatter fields", with `${name}` named as the
            only field the current schema resolves. Note that the default
            scaffold contains no placeholders and the helper is a no-op
            for the canonical install. Keep the load-bearing rule that
            this is the ONLY substitution surface in the package.

### 5. Rewrite the test suite for the new schema

Read: `coding-playbook/workflow/testing`

- [ ] Replace `src/service/parseBrainAide/index.test.ts`. The structure
      stays the same (per-branch `describe` blocks, real-tempdir fixtures,
      `it.each` for parameterized validation), but every fixture and
      assertion needs to swap to the two-field schema. The test file is a
      full rewrite, not a per-block patch — too many fixtures depend on
      the legacy YAML.

      - 5a. **Update the canonical fixture.** Replace the `CANONICAL_BRAIN_AIDE`
            string with the two-field shape from the spec's good example:
            ```
            ---
            name: obsidian
            mcpServerConfig:
              command: npx
              args:
                - "@bitbonsai/mcpvault"
                - "D:/notes/my-vault"
            ---

            ## Prose

            Your brain is an Obsidian vault. ...
            ```
            Update `CANONICAL_CONFIG` to the new `BrainAideConfig` shape:
            `{ name: "obsidian", mcpServerConfig: { command: "npx", args:
            ["@bitbonsai/mcpvault", "D:/notes/my-vault"] } }`.

      - 5b. **Update the `writeBrainAide` helper** to write to
            `.aide/config/brain.aide` (matching the new read path). The
            helper must `mkdir` `.aide/config/` recursively before
