### 6. Verify the parser in isolation, then surface downstream breakage

Read: `coding-playbook/workflow/testing`

- [ ] Run the verification commands and record the results. Two distinct
      checks: the parser's own test suite (must pass cleanly) and the
      project-wide type check (expected to surface downstream consumer
      breakage; that breakage is OUT OF SCOPE for this plan).

      - 6a. Run `npm test -- parseBrainAide` (or the equivalent vitest
            filter that scopes to this module's test file). Every test
            in `src/service/parseBrainAide/index.test.ts` must pass. If
            any fails, fix the parser code (not the test) and re-run —
            the spec is the contract; the tests assert the contract;
            failing tests mean the implementation drifted from the
            spec. Do NOT relax assertions to make tests pass.

      - 6b. Run `npx tsc --noEmit` from the project root. The parser's
            own files must compile cleanly. Downstream consumers
            (`buildBrainState`, `cli/init/writeMcpEntry`, `provisionBrain`,
            and the `obsidianBrainAideTemplate` tests) WILL fail to
            compile because they still read `config.connector` /
            `config.rootPath` / `config.entryFile` / `config.tools` —
            those fields no longer exist on the collapsed
            `BrainAideConfig`. Record the list of failing files in the
            implementor's hand-off message; do NOT modify those files.
            They are governed by their own plan.aide files (the
            orchestrator runs them next in the dependency chain).

            If `tsc` reports errors in any file inside
            `src/service/parseBrainAide/` itself, fix them — that is in
            scope. If it reports errors in any file that imports
            `BrainAideToolMapping` directly (from when the type still
            existed), record those too; those imports get cleaned up in
            the same pass that updates the consumer's field reads.

## Decisions

- **Validation order: required fields first, then deprecated rejection.**
  A user editing brain.aide post-collapse who removed the wrong line
  (deleted `name` instead of `tools`) sees the more-actionable error
  first ("name is required") rather than the more-cryptic deprecated-
  field error. Once they add `name` back, the deprecated-field check
  surfaces on the next parse. This matches the existing one-error-per-
  parse pattern (the parser short-circuits on the first failure) and
  keeps the user moving forward through one structured failure at a
  time.

- **Deprecated fields listed in fixed order, not user-file order.**
  Returning `"deprecated fields: rootPath, tools"` in deprecated-set
  order (`connector, rootPath, entryFile, tools`) gives deterministic
  output regardless of which order the user wrote them in their YAML.
  Tests can assert on the exact reason string; remediation prose can
  quote it without surprises. The user's file ordering is not load-
  bearing here — what matters is which fields are present, not where.

- **All deprecated fields named in one reason, not first-found.** The
  reason string lists every stale field found, not just the first one.
  Surfacing them all in one parse means the user fixes them all in one
  edit and re-runs the parser once, rather than the dance of "remove
  rootPath, re-parse, remove tools, re-parse, remove entryFile". This
  costs one set-intersection at parse time and saves multiple round-
  trips at edit time. The spec's good-example for the rejection branch
  explicitly shows multi-field output (`"deprecated fields: rootPath,
  tools"`), confirming this is the intended UX.

- **`BrainAideToolMapping` is deleted, not retained for future use.**
  The type's only consumer is the deleted `tools` field. Keeping it
  exported as "future-proofing" violates the parent intent spec's
  "schema must justify each field by naming a real runtime consumer"
  rule by analogy — a type with no consumer is type-system theater.
  When (if) tools-mapping returns, the type can be re-introduced
  alongside the field that needs it. Today: delete.

- **`BrainAideMcpServerConfig` survives.** Unlike `BrainAideToolMapping`,
  it has a current consumer (`BrainAideConfig.mcpServerConfig`). Keeping
  it as a named alias rather than inlining `{ command, args }` preserves
  the JSDoc-documented semantic detail that `args` may contain
  pre-interpolation placeholders — that comment lives on the type, not
  on every site that reads `mcpServerConfig`.

- **`parseBrainAideFromString` stays, with no signature change.** Four
  consumers import it (`writeMcpEntry`, `provisionBrain`,
  `obsidianBrainAideTemplate` tests, and the parser's own tests).
  Removing it would expand this plan into a four-module refactor; the
  spec doesn't ask for that. The byte-input variant continues to share
  the validation block with the disk-reading default export — the
  refactor that hoisted steps 2-6 into the shared helper still applies.

- **The interpolation engine stays generic.** Even though `${name}` is
  the only resolvable key today, the substitution map is built from
  the parsed frontmatter object (one entry: `{ name }`), not hard-coded.
  When the schema grows a new top-level field, adding it to the
  substitution map is a one-line edit. This matches the parent spec's
  "the engine generalizes naturally if the schema ever grows — no
  field-specific privilege" rule.

- **The read-path move is a one-line constant change, not a
  configurable parameter.** The parser hard-codes
  `.aide/config/brain.aide` because the canonical doc declares it as
  the contractual location. Making it configurable would re-introduce
  the same multi-source-of-truth problem the user-owned-`.aide/config/`
  decision was meant to eliminate. If a future host needs a different
  location, that's a methodology change at the doc level first, then
  a parser change — never a runtime knob.

- **Test rewrites do not preserve legacy fixtures.** A test rewrite
  rather than a per-block patch keeps the file readable: every fixture
  in the file matches the current schema, so a future reader doesn't
  have to guess which tests are testing legacy behavior versus current.
  Keeping legacy fixtures around as "regression tests" against an
  unsupported schema would be exactly the validation theater the spec
  rejects.

- **Step 6 records but does not fix downstream breakage.** The four
  downstream modules (`buildBrainState`, `writeMcpEntry`,
  `provisionBrain`, `obsidianBrainAideTemplate`) each have their own
  `.aide` and (eventually) `plan.aide` for the schema-collapse cascade.
  Fixing them inside this plan would expand this plan's scope past
  the single-module scoping rule. The verify step's job is to make
  the breakage visible — the orchestrator picks up those plans next.
